# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AURIX runtime configuration — the Flutter app
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill it in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env        (macOS / Linux)
#   Copy-Item .env.example .env (Windows PowerShell)
#
# `.env` is git-ignored.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DATABASE IS NOT CONFIGURED HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AURIX's data lives in MongoDB, and this app never touches MongoDB. It talks
# to the AURIX API in `web/`, which holds the connection string and is the
# only process that can reach the cluster.
#
# `MONGODB_URI` therefore belongs in `web/.env.local` and must NEVER appear here.
# A connection string bundled into a mobile app is a connection string
# published to everyone who installs it — the APK is a zip file, and `.env` is
# an asset inside it. No amount of obfuscation changes that.
#
# What is configured below is a URL. It is not a secret; it is the address of
# a public HTTP service that authenticates every request it serves.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There are two blocks below and they are not equally important:
#
#   1. AURIX_ENV          — required. AURIX cannot sign in without it.
#   2. SPOTIFY_*          — optional. Enables one feature: Settings -> Import music.
#
# A build with the API block filled in and the Spotify block empty is a
# complete, working AURIX. Sign-in, playlists, liked songs, history, search and
# the whole theme system all work; the import screen lists Spotify as
# unavailable and says why.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ===========================================================================
# 1. THE AURIX API — required
# ===========================================================================
# Start the backend first:
#
#   cd web
#   cp .env.example .env.local  # then fill in MONGODB_URI and the JWT secrets
#   npm install
#   npm run indexes             # required once per environment — the unique
#                               # indexes are the schema, not an optimisation
#   npm run dev                 # listens on :3000, admin portal at /admin
#
# Then point the app at it. One value does that.
#
# ---- The switch -----------------------------------------------------------
# AURIX_ENV picks the deployment, and lib/core/config/api_config.dart holds the
# address each name means:
#
#   production    https://aurix-iota-tawny.vercel.app
#   development   http://localhost:3000  (http://10.0.2.2:3000 on the Android
#                                         emulator, which is its alias for the
#                                         host machine)
#
# Changing environment is therefore this one line, in this one file. No Dart
# source contains a backend URL.
#
# There is deliberately no default in a DEBUG build: a silent fallback would
# make the in-app setup screen — the one surface that tells you what to add —
# unreachable on exactly the build that needs it. A RELEASE build with nothing
# set falls back to production, because a shipped binary has no setup screen
# worth showing.
#
# For CI, pass it as a define instead of shipping a .env:
#   flutter build apk --dart-define=AURIX_ENV=production
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AURIX_ENV=production

# ---- Explicit overrides — optional, and they win over AURIX_ENV ------------
# The one case that needs them is a DEVELOPMENT build on a PHYSICAL DEVICE.
# `localhost` means three different things depending on where the app runs, and
# on a real phone it means the phone: the request never leaves the device.
#
#   AURIX_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.42:3000          # the host's LAN address
#   AURIX_API_BASE_URL_ANDROID=http://127.0.0.1:3000     # with `adb reverse tcp:3000 tcp:3000`
#   AURIX_API_BASE_URL_WEB=http://localhost:3000         # add this origin to CORS_ORIGINS
#
# Leave all three blank in production: one https address serves every platform.
#
# In production the URL must be https. Every request carries a bearer token,
# and a token on an unencrypted connection is a token anyone on the network
# has — the app logs a warning at boot when it sees http against a non-loopback
# host.
AURIX_API_BASE_URL=
AURIX_API_BASE_URL_ANDROID=
AURIX_API_BASE_URL_WEB=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing in with Google, Apple, Facebook or GitHub
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is NO client id here and there is certainly no client secret, and that
# is the whole design rather than an omission.
#
# AURIX's social sign-in is run by the API, not by the app. The app asks the
# server to start a flow, the browser goes to the provider, the provider
# returns to THE SERVER, and the server — which holds the client secret —
# trades the code for a token and hands the app a single-use grant. So the four
# providers are configured in `web/.env.local` and nowhere else, and a client
# secret never exists inside the binary. (Compare section 2 below: the Spotify
# import runs in the app, so its public client id does live here.)
#
# The only thing this build needs to know is the address the API should send
# the browser back to at the very end.
#
# THIS VALUE MUST APPEAR IN THREE PLACES, spelled identically:
#   1. here,
#   2. OAUTH_APP_REDIRECTS in web/.env.local — the server refuses to hand a grant
#      to an address it has not been told about, which is what stops it being
#      an open redirector with a session attached, and
#   3. the CallbackActivity intent filter in
#      android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml.
#
# Deliberately a different host from SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI. Two pending
# authorizations competing for one URI is resolved by Android with a chooser or
# an arbitrary pick, and the losing flow hangs until it times out.
AURIX_LOGIN_REDIRECT_URI=aurix://login-callback

# Flutter Web only. Nothing can redirect a browser tab to a custom scheme, so
# the web build comes back through the same `auth.html` bridge page the Spotify
# flow uses. Leave it empty to derive `<origin>/auth.html` from wherever the app
# is being served, which is what makes `flutter run -d chrome` work unedited.
AURIX_LOGIN_REDIRECT_URI_WEB=

# ===========================================================================
# 2. SPOTIFY — optional
# ===========================================================================
# Two separate things, both optional, both off by default:
#
#   * Import.   Settings -> Import music -> Spotify reads the account's
#               playlists once and copies them into Firestore. Metadata and
#               references only: no audio is downloaded, cached or stored.
#   * Playback. Spotify App Remote and Spotify Connect are currently the only
#               way AURIX can play a full track, for tracks that carry a
#               Spotify id. See `MusicPlaybackService`.
#
# Leave the block empty and both are simply unavailable. Nothing else changes.
#
# AURIX uses Authorization Code + PKCE, the flow Spotify documents for public
# clients. PKCE needs NO client secret, so there is intentionally no
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET key here and none anywhere in the shipped binary. If a
# guide tells you to embed a client secret in a mobile app, that guide is wrong.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Your Spotify application's Client ID. Public by design under PKCE.
# Copy it from Dashboard -> your app -> Settings -> Basic Information.
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id_here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redirect URIs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Spotify matches the redirect URI EXACTLY. Register every value you use, on
# every platform you build for, in:
#   Dashboard -> your app -> Settings -> Edit -> Redirect URIs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Android / iOS / desktop. Custom schemes are still supported by Spotify for
# mobile clients (see the Feb 2025 security-requirements post); they are the
# documented approach for a public PKCE client.
#
# The scheme is also wired into AndroidManifest.xml and Info.plist —
# if you change it here, change it there too.
SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI=aurix://auth-callback

# Spotify App Remote (Android/iOS full-track playback). A SEPARATE dashboard
# Redirect URI from the one above. Must match redirectSchemeName and
# redirectHostName in android/app/build.gradle.kts.
SPOTIFY_APP_REMOTE_REDIRECT_URI=aurix://spotify-remote

# The custom URL scheme part of SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI (no "://").
# flutter_web_auth_2 uses this to know when to close the auth browser tab.
# Ignored on Flutter Web, which matches on a postMessage handshake instead.
SPOTIFY_CALLBACK_SCHEME=aurix

# Flutter Web only. A browser cannot be redirected to a custom scheme, so web
# needs a real http(s) URL pointing at the auth.html shipped in web/.
#
# PIN THIS. Blank means "derive <origin>/auth.html from wherever the app is
# being served", and `flutter run -d chrome` takes a random OS port unless
# --web-port says otherwise — giving a different redirect_uri on every launch
# (http://127.0.0.1:51066/auth.html, then :52310, …). None of them can be
# registered, and Spotify answers "redirect_uri: Not matching configuration".
#
# Two rules Spotify enforces, both of which cost an afternoon if missed:
#   * `localhost` is NOT accepted. Use the loopback literal 127.0.0.1.
#   * Anything that is not a loopback address must be https.
#
# Run web with a matching port, and register this exact string in the
# dashboard:
#   flutter run -d chrome --web-port 8080
#
# For a deployed build, set it to the real origin instead, e.g.:
#   SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI_WEB=https://aurix.example.com/auth.html
SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI_WEB=http://127.0.0.1:8080/auth.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional token-exchange proxy — ALL BLANK = OFF, which is correct for PKCE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AURIX needs no backend. Leave these blank unless you are actually running
# the optional proxy described in the README; blank means tokens are exchanged
# directly with accounts.spotify.com, which is right for a public PKCE client.
#
# If you DO run one locally it needs a different address per platform, because
# "localhost" means a different machine on each:
#
#   AUTH_PROXY_BASE_URL_WEB     browser on this PC   -> http://127.0.0.1:<port>
#   AUTH_PROXY_BASE_URL_MOBILE  physical phone       -> http://<PC-LAN-IP>:<port>
#                               Android emulator     -> http://10.0.2.2:<port>
#                               iOS simulator        -> http://127.0.0.1:<port>
#
# A phone's own loopback is the phone, so 127.0.0.1 there never reaches your
# PC. AURIX warns at boot if a native build is pointed at loopback.
#
# The platform-specific key wins when set; AUTH_PROXY_BASE_URL is the shared
# fallback for deployments where a single URL serves every platform.
#
# For a phone to reach a backend on your PC's LAN IP, that backend must bind
# to 0.0.0.0 (not 127.0.0.1), the port must be allowed through the host
# firewall, and both devices must be on the same network.
AUTH_PROXY_BASE_URL=
AUTH_PROXY_BASE_URL_WEB=
AUTH_PROXY_BASE_URL_MOBILE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (removed) Firebase
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AURIX no longer uses Firebase. Identity, the profile, playlists, liked
# songs, play history, the shared catalogue and the theme configuration all
# live in MongoDB behind the API in web/ — see section 1.
#
# FIREBASE_* keys left over from an earlier install are inert. Nothing reads
# them and they can be deleted.
#
# Note what does NOT carry over: Firebase Auth accounts. Firebase does not
# export password hashes in a form another system can verify, so existing
# users have to register once more. There is no migration script for this
# and no way to write one — see docs/MONGODB_MIGRATION.md.


# ===========================================================================
# 3. YOUTUBE / YOUTUBE MUSIC — playlist link import
# ===========================================================================
# Set YOUTUBE_API_KEY and pasting a YouTube or YouTube Music playlist link
# works. Leave it empty and AURIX still runs — the import screen reports the
# YouTube option as unconfigured and Spotify import is unaffected.
#
# ---- Getting the key ------------------------------------------------------
#
#   1. https://console.cloud.google.com → create (or pick) a project.
#   2. APIs & Services → Library → enable "YouTube Data API v3".
#   3. APIs & Services → Credentials → Create credentials → API key.
#   4. Paste it below.
#
# ---- Restrict it ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# The key is not a secret in the way a client secret is: it identifies the
# project for quota accounting and authorises nothing on its own. It is still
# shipped inside the app binary, so restrict it or someone who extracts it can
# spend this project's quota:
#
#   * API restrictions      → "YouTube Data API v3" only.
#   * Application restrict. → your Android package + SHA-1, and/or your iOS
#                             bundle id.
#
# ---- Quota, which is the limit you will actually hit -----------------------
#
# A new project gets 10,000 units/day. Importing one playlist costs roughly:
#
#   playlists.list        1 unit
#   playlistItems.list    1 unit per page of 50 songs
#   videos.list           1 unit per batch of 50 songs (durations)
#
# So a 200-song playlist is about 9 units, and the daily quota is a few
# hundred imports. A quota failure surfaces as "AURIX has reached its daily
# limit with that service", never as a raw error.
#
YOUTUBE_API_KEY=

# ---- Optional: private playlists ------------------------------------------
#
# The API key above reaches public playlists, which is what a pasted link
# normally is. A user's *private* playlists need OAuth with `youtube.readonly`
# and the user's own consent — pasting a link to one currently reports "That
# playlist is private."
#
# Setting these does not by itself enable private import; the OAuth flow is
# not implemented. They are here so the configuration is in place for it.
#
# YOUTUBE_CLIENT_ID=your_google_oauth_client_id
# YOUTUBE_REDIRECT_URI=aurix://youtube-callback
